@startuml bedrock_block
title K9-AIF with AWS Bedrock AgentCore: three integration paths

rectangle "AWS Bedrock AgentCore" {
  rectangle "Runtime" as RT {
    rectangle "Path A: K9-AIF application\n(BedrockAgentCoreApp entrypoint\n-> Router -> Orchestrator -> Squad)" as K9App
  }
  rectangle "Harness\n(managed agent loop)" as Harness
  rectangle "Deployed agent\n(any framework)" as Other
  rectangle "Gateway\n(Cedar policy engine)" as GW
  rectangle "Memory" as Mem
  rectangle "Identity" as Id
  rectangle "Bedrock models\n(Converse API)" as Bedrock
}

rectangle "K9-AIF" {
  rectangle "Path B: BedrockAgentCoreOrchestratorAdapter\n(harness mode / runtime mode)" as Adapter
  rectangle "Shield governance\n+ tool gate + HIL" as Shield
  rectangle "Path C: Shield interceptor\n(Lambda, REQUEST / RESPONSE)" as Interceptor
}

rectangle "Tools\n(MCP servers, APIs, Lambda)" as Tools

K9App --> Mem : AgentCoreMemorySessionStore
K9App --> Bedrock : BedrockLLM via Model Router
K9App --> Id : inbound JWT -> OIDC principal
K9App --> Shield : in-process

Adapter --> Harness : harness mode
Adapter --> Other : runtime mode
Adapter --> Shield

Harness --> GW : server-side tools
Other --> GW
GW --> Interceptor : every tool call and result
Interceptor --> Shield
GW --> Tools : allowed by Cedar and Shield

note bottom of Adapter
  harness mode: inline tool calls come back
  to the adapter (to be verified live).
  runtime mode: boundary governance only.
end note

note bottom of GW
  Cedar decides who may call which tool.
  Shield checks what the call carries:
  injection, PII, arguments against records.
end note
@enduml
